layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | Buildyboy is built from a few simple parts. Once you know what each one does, every screen in the app makes sense,
    | and so do most problems. This page explains them in plain words, from the computer you type on to the phone in
    | your pocket.

  h2#the-picture The picture
  div(data-ui="grid" data-cols="1" data-cols-md="3" data-gap="4")
    +card(title="1 · Your computers" variant="outlined")
      @body
        p Claude Code sessions, each with the Buildyboy plugin reporting what happens. Optionally the runner, which starts jobs.
    +card(title="2 · Your server" variant="outlined")
      @body
        p
          code {{ site.host }}
          | stores everything, serves the app, holds requests that wait for you and sends notifications.
    +card(title="3 · Your browser and phone" variant="outlined")
      @body
        p The Floor, the Inbox and notifications. Your answers and new jobs travel back the same way.
  p
    | Information flows one way most of the time: your sessions report what they do, and the server stores it and shows
    | it to you. It only flows back when you answer something (a permission, a question, a message) or launch a job.

  h2#server The server
  p
    | The server is one program and one database file running on a machine you control (for example, inside WSL on your
    | home PC, published as
    code {{ site.url }}
    | ). It does four things:
  ul
    li receives events from your computers and stores them,
    li serves the Buildyboy web app you are using (and this manual),
    li holds requests that are waiting for your answer and passes your answer back,
    li sends notifications to your devices.
  p
    | The server never runs Claude Code or any command itself. It only records, displays and relays. Anything that runs
    | happens on your own computers.

  h2#machines Machines and pairing
  p
    | A
    strong machine
    | is one computer (or one WSL installation) where you use Claude Code. Before Buildyboy can see it, you
    strong pair
    | it: you create a short one-time code on the
    strong Machines
    | page and run an installer on the computer. The installer saves the computer's own access key in a folder called
    code ~/.buildyboy
    | and installs the Buildyboy plugin into Claude Code.
  p
    | Each machine has its own key. You can rename a machine, give it a new key, or cut it off for good (revoke it) at
    | any time from the
    a(href="/docs/machines") Machines
    | page.

  h2#sessions Sessions
  p
    | A
    strong session
    | is one conversation with Claude Code: everything from starting
    code claude
    | in a folder until you quit. Buildyboy gives each session a card on the
    a(href="/docs/floor") Floor
    | and a page of its own with the full timeline. Sessions are grouped by machine and by
    strong project
    | (the git repository or folder they run in).
  p A session always has one of these statuses:
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Status
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") What it means
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="success") Working
          td(data-part="td") Claude is busy: thinking, running a tool or a subagent.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="warning") Waiting on you
          td(data-part="td") Claude stopped and needs you: a permission prompt, a question or a plan to approve.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="primary") Idle
          td(data-part="td") The turn is over and Claude is waiting for your next prompt. Nothing is wrong.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="primary") Compacting
          td(data-part="td") Claude Code is summarising the conversation to free up context space.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="warning") Stale
          td(data-part="td") It was working, but no event has arrived for 10 minutes. It may be stuck, or the computer may be asleep.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            span(data-ui="badge" data-variant="destructive") Error
          td(data-part="td") The turn failed, for example because of a rate limit or an API error.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            span(data-ui="badge") Ended
          td(data-part="td") The session was closed. Resuming it with claude --resume brings it back.

  h2#hooks The plugin and its hooks
  p
    | Claude Code can call small helper programs at fixed moments, called
    strong hooks
    | : when a session starts, before and after each tool, when it asks for permission, when a turn ends, and so on. The
    | Buildyboy plugin registers one hook script for these moments. Each time it runs, it sends a short event to your
    | server.
  p The hook is built to stay out of your way:
  ul
    li
      strong It fails open.
      | If the server is down or slow, the hook gives up within about a second, prints nothing and lets Claude Code carry
      | on exactly as if Buildyboy did not exist.
    li
      strong It keeps what it could not send.
      | Events that could not be delivered are saved in
      code ~/.buildyboy/spool.jsonl
      | and sent later, so history has no holes. The spool is capped at 50 MB.
    li
      strong It backs off.
      | After a failed call it stops trying for 60 seconds, so a dead server costs you nothing.
    li
      strong It masks secrets.
      | API keys, tokens and the contents of
      code .env
      | files are replaced with
      code [REDACTED]
      | on the server before anything is stored. See
      a(href="/docs/security") Security and privacy
      | .
  p
    | With walk mode off, the hook only reports. It never changes what Claude does and never makes it wait.

  h2#walk-mode Walk mode
  p
    strong Walk mode
    | is the switch that lets the server hold Claude for you. When it is on, the hook does not just report a permission
    | prompt, a question or a plan; it asks the server and waits for your answer, which you give from the
    a(href="/docs/inbox") Inbox
    | or straight from a notification. At the end of each turn Claude also waits a few minutes for your next instruction.
  p
    | If you do not answer in time, nothing breaks: the request falls back to Claude Code's normal behaviour (the prompt
    | appears in the terminal). Walk mode can be set for everything, per machine, or per session. All of it is explained
    | in
    a(href="/docs/walk-mode") Walk mode
    | .

  h2#runner-and-jobs The runner and jobs
  p
    | Watching sessions you started yourself needs nothing more. To
    strong start
    | work from Buildyboy (for example from your phone at night), a machine also needs the
    strong runner
    | : a small background program that keeps a connection open to the server and, when you launch a
    strong job
    | , starts
    code claude -p
    | with your prompt in the folder you chose.
  p
    | For safety the runner only works inside the folders you list in its own settings file on that machine, called the
    strong allowed roots
    | . The server cannot change that list. See
    a(href="/docs/runner") Setting up the runner
    | and
    a(href="/docs/jobs") Jobs and schedules
    | .

  h2#harness The harness prompt
  p
    | Unattended runs work best with a clear, complete instruction. Buildyboy ships one, the
    strong harness prompt
    | : a template with a
    strong Goal
    | , a
    strong Done when
    | list of checks, a
    strong Scope
    | and a few switches, followed by fixed advice on how to work, test, commit and keep a
    code PROGRESS.md
    | file. The
    a(href="/docs/composer") Composer
    | helps you fill it in, warns you about gaps, and keeps your prompts in a library.

  h2#data Where your data lives
  ul
    li
      strong On the server:
      | one SQLite database file holding sessions, events, settings, the password hash, machine keys (hashed) and push
      | subscriptions. It is backed up every night. See
      a(href="/docs/server#backups") Backups
      | .
    li
      strong On each machine:
      | the
      code ~/.buildyboy
      | folder with the access key (
      code config.json
      | ), the plugin, the spool, and the runner's settings (
      code runner.json
      | ) if you use it. Your Claude Code settings file is never touched.
    li
      strong In your browser:
      | only your login cookie and a few view preferences (theme, Floor filters, card positions).
